Features 

User management with roles: ADMIN, INSTRUCTOR, STUDENT 

Instructor workflows: create classes, manage roster, view attendance 

Student workflows: list classes, check-in/out with optional geolocation 

Secure messaging (threads + messages) for instructors and students 

JWT-based authentication with route role guards 

DB schema & seed scripts for demo data in db/ 

Dockerfile for containerized backend 

 

Architecture & Project Layout 

Top-level layout: 

backend/ — Express API with ESM modules 

server.js — App entry point 

routes/ — API route handlers (auth, classes, enrollments, attendance, chat, users, geo) 

middleware/ — auth helpers 

geo/ — IP geolocation helper (optional; Abstract API) 

db.js + config.js — DB pool and config (dotenv) 

Dockerfile — container image for the backend 

student-tracker-frontend/ — Static frontend files 

pages/ — HTML pages (login, admin, instructor, student, classes, chat, checkin, class) 

js/ — client JS (api, auth, ui, chat) 

styles.css — app look & feel 

db/ — schema.sql and seed.sql for database setup 

DEPLOYMENT.md — deployment and dev guidance (keep this as canonical deploy reference) 

 

Quick Start — Local Development 

These instructions assume you have Node.js (v18+) and Docker (optional) available. 

1) Start a PostgreSQL database (Docker, recommended) 

Open PowerShell and run: 

# create a Postgres container (replace password/db names as needed) 
docker run -d --name st-postgres -e POSTGRES_USER=st_user -e POSTGRES_PASSWORD=st_pass -e POSTGRES_DB=studenttracker -p 5432:5432 postgres:15 
  

Or use your existing Postgres instance and point DATABASE_URL accordingly. 

2) Prepare backend environment 

Create a .env file in backend/ with at least: 

DATABASE_URL=postgres://st_user:st_pass@localhost:5432/studenttracker 
JWT_SECRET=replace-with-a-secure-random-string 
PORT=3000 
# OPTIONAL: ABSTRACT_API_KEY=your_abstract_api_key (for IP geolocation) 
NODE_ENV=development 
  

Do not commit .env. The repo already includes .gitignore which excludes it. 

3) Initialize the database schema & seed data 

Using psql run db/schema.sql and db/seed.sql: 

# Example using psql installed locally 
psql "postgres://st_user:st_pass@localhost:5432/studenttracker" -f db/schema.sql 
psql "postgres://st_user:st_pass@localhost:5432/studenttracker" -f db/seed.sql 
  

If you don't have psql on the host, run inside the Postgres container: 

# copy files into container and run them 
docker cp db/schema.sql st-postgres:/tmp/schema.sql 
docker cp db/seed.sql st-postgres:/tmp/seed.sql 
docker exec -it st-postgres bash -c "psql -U st_user -d studenttracker -f /tmp/schema.sql && psql -U st_user -d studenttracker -f /tmp/seed.sql" 
  

4) Start the backend in development mode 

cd backend 
npm install 
npm run dev 
  

This uses nodemon to restart the server on file changes. 

The API starts on http://localhost:3000 by default. You can verify with: 

curl http://localhost:3000/api/health 
  

5) Serve frontend files 

The frontend is static files. For quick local testing there's no build step — serve the files with a static server or open the student-tracker-frontend/pages/index.html in your browser. 

Quick example using npm's serve (or npx serve): 

# From repo root or student-tracker-frontend/ 
cd student-tracker-frontend 
npx serve . -l 5000 
# open http://localhost:5000/pages/index.html 
  

If the frontend runs on a different origin than the API, the backend is CORS-enabled by default. 

 

Environment Variables 

Add these to backend/.env (some are required): 

DATABASE_URL — Postgres connection string (required) 

JWT_SECRET — strong secret for signing JWTs (required) 

PORT — API port (default 3000) 

ABSTRACT_API_KEY — Optional: key for IP geolocation (used by geo endpoint) 

NODE_ENV — development or production (default development) 

Refer to backend/config.js for how these are used. 

 

Database 

Schema and seed SQL live under db/: 

db/schema.sql — DDL for the app schema 

db/seed.sql — Inserts demo users, a sample class, enrollments, and a sample chat thread 

The schema uses types and constraints to support: users, classes, enrollments, attendance, threads, messages, and read receipts. 

 

Running With Docker (optional) 

Backend container 

The backend Dockerfile is located in backend/ and exposes port 3001 in the container. You can run the container and map to a host port. Ensure the container can reach a Postgres database or use a docker-compose with a Postgres service. 

cd backend 
# build image 
docker build -t studenttracker-backend . 
# run (assuming network and DATABASE_URL are set) 
docker run -p 3000:3001 --env-file .env --name st-api studenttracker-backend 
  

For a local compose setup, see DEPLOYMENT.md for a recommended docker-compose snippet. 

 

API Endpoints (Overview) 

Most endpoints live under /api/*. Important ones include: 

Auth 

POST /api/auth/login — login (returns JWT and user) 

POST /api/auth/register — create user and sign in 

Users (admin-only for the full list) 

GET /api/users — list users (ADMIN only) 

POST /api/users — create user (ADMIN only) 

GET /api/users/students — list students 

GET /api/users/instructors — list instructors 

GET /api/users/people — students + instructors for selection 

Classes 

GET /api/classes — instructor's classes (query: instructorId) 

GET /api/classes/mine — student's classes 

GET /api/classes/:id — class details 

POST /api/classes — create a class (INSTRUCTOR/ADMIN) 

DELETE /api/classes/:id — delete a class 

Enrollments 

GET /api/enrollments/:classId — roster (INSTRUCTOR/ADMIN) 

POST /api/enrollments — enroll student 

DELETE /api/enrollments — unenroll 

Attendance 

POST /api/attendance/checkin — student check-in 

POST /api/attendance/checkout — student check-out 

GET /api/attendance/:classId — instructor fetch attendance for a class 

GET /api/attendance/my/:classId — student fetch my attendance 

Chat 

GET /api/chat/threads — all threads user participates in 

GET /api/chat/threads/:id/messages — messages in a thread 

POST /api/chat/threads/:id/messages — send a message 

POST /api/chat/threads — create thread (DIRECT or GROUP) 

Geolocation 

GET /api/geo/ip — Lookup IP geolocation (optional; uses Abstract API key) 

These are enforced by role-based middleware in backend/middleware/auth.js. 

 

Frontend Notes 

The frontend is a static set of pages in student-tracker-frontend/pages/. 

Primary JS utilities live in student-tracker-frontend/js/: api.js, auth.js, ui.js, chat utilities. 

Client-side login stores JWT in localStorage under st_token and user metadata under st_user. 

The UI expects API to be available at http://localhost:3000 by default, set in student-tracker-frontend/js/api.js. 

To run the frontend while developing the backend locally, serve the static site as noted above. 

 

Contributing 

Contributions are welcome. Some ideas: 

-Add unit tests and integration tests for API routes 

-Improve UI/UX, accessibility, and layout 

-Add pagination and filtering on large datasets 

-Add strong validation and rate limiting on API 

-Add integration tests and CI pipeline 

When contributing, please follow a pull request workflow and include a description, testing steps and any new environment variables or schema migrations. 

 

Security & Secrets 

Never commit .env or any secrets to source control. 

Use strong JWT_SECRET values in production and rotate when needed. 

Store production secrets securely (Azure Key Vault, AWS Secrets Manager, etc.) and set TLS/SSL settings for PG connections (NODE_ENV=production flips PG_SSL on). 

 

License 

This project is provided as-is for demo and learning purposes. Please see the LICENSE file if present or add an appropriate license if using this in production. 

 

More Info 
For troubleshooting, check backend logs and ensure the DB is initialized with db/schema.sql and db/seed.sql. 